<p>Selling a house with foundation problems in Houston is common and entirely doable. You have three realistic paths: repair first and sell with an engineer report and transferable warranty, sell to a financed buyer with a price cut or credit, or sell as-is to a cash buyer. The right choice depends on what an independent engineer says, who your likely buyers are, and the net math for each option.</p>

<h2>Why foundation problems are so common in Houston</h2>
<p>Much of the Houston area sits on clay soils of the Beaumont Formation. Geologists and engineers classify much of that clay as having moderate to high shrink-swell potential. When the soil gets wet, it swells. When it dries during a drought or a hot summer, it shrinks. A slab sitting on top of that soil moves with it, often unevenly.</p>
<p>Poor drainage, large trees close to the house, plumbing leaks under the slab and inconsistent watering all make the swings worse. That is why Houston buyers, inspectors and lenders pay close attention to foundations, and why so many local homes have had some foundation work at some point.</p>
<p>The good news: because foundation repair is routine here, Houston buyers are used to seeing it. A documented, warrantied repair is often a minor issue in negotiations. Unknown or undocumented movement is what scares buyers away.</p>

<h2>Signs of foundation trouble buyers will notice</h2>
<p>Before you list, walk the house the way a buyer's inspector will. Common warning signs include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Diagonal cracks in drywall running from the corners of doors and windows</li>
<li>Doors that stick, swing open or closed on their own, or no longer latch</li>
<li>Windows that bind or show gaps at the frame</li>
<li>Stair-step cracks in exterior brick or mortar</li>
<li>Gaps between walls and ceilings, or between baseboards and the floor</li>
<li>Sloping or uneven floors, or cracked floor tile</li>
<li>Cracks in the exposed slab edge or separation of the garage slab</li>
</ul>
<p>Not every crack means the foundation needs repair. Some minor cracking from concrete shrinkage and normal seasonal movement is common. That judgment call is exactly why you want a professional evaluation rather than a guess. A laser distance measure helps you document the location and size of cracks or gaps for your records and for the engineer.</p>
[[product:laser-measure]]

<h2>Engineer report vs. contractor bid: get both, in the right order</h2>
<p>Sellers often call a foundation repair company first because the inspection is free. That gives you one company's opinion and a sales proposal. Buyers and their agents know that.</p>
<p>A stronger approach:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Hire an independent structural engineer.</strong> Use a professional engineer licensed in Texas who does not sell repairs. The engineer evaluates the foundation, often takes elevation readings, and writes a report stating whether repair is needed and what it should include.</li>
<li><strong>Get two or three contractor bids on the engineer's scope.</strong> Comparing bids against the same plan is apples to apples. Contractors who bid on their own recommendations can propose very different scopes, which makes bids hard to compare.</li>
<li><strong>Have the engineer review the completed work.</strong> A final letter confirming the repair was done to plan is one of the most persuasive documents you can give a buyer.</li>
</ol>
<p>In the City of Houston, a residential slab foundation repair requires a building permit, and the permit application needs foundation plans and details from a Texas-licensed engineer showing the location, quantity and type of supports. Properties in certain flood zones need additional documents, such as an itemized engineer estimate or notarized repair contract plus an appraisal. Suburbs and unincorporated areas have their own rules, so check with your city.</p>
<p>Even if you plan to sell as-is, an engineer report helps. It turns an unknown into a defined problem that buyers can price, instead of a worst-case guess.</p>

<h2>Common repair types: slab and pier-and-beam</h2>
<p>Your engineer will recommend the method. These are the ones you will hear about most in Houston:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Method</th><th>Foundation type</th><th>How it works</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Pressed concrete pilings</td><td>Slab</td><td>Precast concrete cylinders are hydraulically pressed into the ground beneath the slab, using the weight of the house for resistance, until they stop advancing. The slab is then lifted and supported.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steel piers</td><td>Slab</td><td>Steel pipe sections are driven deep under the foundation. Contractors often use them for heavier structures or where concrete pilings cannot reach firm soil.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drilled (bell-bottom) piers</td><td>Slab</td><td>A shaft is drilled, flared at the bottom into a bell shape, and filled with reinforced concrete. Supporters point out that the depth and shape can be specified and inspected.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shimming, added supports and beam replacement</td><td>Pier and beam</td><td>The crawl space lets crews re-level the house, add or replace piers and supports, and replace damaged beams or sills.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Repair costs vary widely with the number of piers, access, plumbing issues found during the work, and whether interior tunneling is needed. Get local bids rather than relying on averages you see online. Ask every contractor whether a plumbing test is recommended, because under-slab leaks can cause or follow foundation movement.</p>

<h3>Transferable warranties</h3>
<p>Most Houston foundation repair companies offer a warranty, and many are transferable to a new owner. Terms differ. Some require a transfer fee, written notice within a set time after closing, or a re-inspection. Find your paperwork early and read the transfer section. A valid transferable warranty with the engineer's final letter is the single most reassuring package you can offer a buyer.</p>
<p>If you agree in the TREC resale contract to make repairs, the contract requires them to be done by licensed or commercially engaged professionals before closing, with documentation of the scope of work and payment, and you must arrange, at your expense, the transfer of any transferable warranties to the buyer.</p>

<h2>Your disclosure duty on the TREC seller's disclosure</h2>
<p>Most Texas resales require a seller's disclosure notice under Texas Property Code Section 5.008. The current TREC Seller's Disclosure Notice (form 55-1) asks about foundation issues in several places:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Known defects</strong> in the "Foundation/Slab(s)" and other structural components.</li>
<li><strong>Conditions you are aware of</strong>, including "Previous Structural or Roof Repair," "Improper Drainage," and "Landfill, Settling, Soil Movement, Fault Lines."</li>
<li><strong>Any item in need of repair</strong> that you know about.</li>
</ul>
<p>A past repair is still a previous structural repair, even if the foundation is now within tolerance. Disclose it, describe what was done and when, and offer the engineer reports, permit and warranty. Under the TREC resale contract, a buyer who receives the disclosure after signing can terminate within seven days of receiving it. Late or incomplete disclosure about the foundation is one of the fastest ways to lose a deal, or to invite a fraud claim after closing. Our guide to the <a href="/blog/texas-seller-disclosure-notice/">Texas seller disclosure notice</a> walks through the form, and the current version is posted on the <a href="https://www.trec.texas.gov/agency-information/contracts">TREC contracts page</a>.</p>

<h2>How FHA, VA and conventional lenders react</h2>
<p>Your buyer's loan type affects how a foundation problem plays out.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>FHA:</strong> HUD requires the appraiser to report defective conditions, including evidence of continuing settlement or other conditions affecting the structural soundness of the house. The lender decides which repairs are required and may order an inspection by a qualified professional. If correction is not feasible, the lender must reject the property.</li>
<li><strong>VA:</strong> The VA appraisal checks the home against VA minimum property requirements, which aim for a home that is safe, sound and sanitary. Visible structural problems can lead to required repairs or further inspection before the loan closes.</li>
<li><strong>Conventional:</strong> Requirements vary by lender and appraiser, but obvious structural defects can still trigger repair conditions.</li>
</ul>
<p>The TREC contract addresses this directly. Unless the parties agree otherwise in writing, neither buyer nor seller has to pay for lender-required repairs. If no one agrees to pay, the contract terminates and the buyer gets the earnest money back. If lender-required repairs cost more than 5% of the sales price, the buyer may terminate. Active, unrepaired movement narrows your buyer pool to cash and some conventional buyers. A completed, documented repair opens the door to everyone.</p>

<h2>Your three options and the pricing math</h2>
<h3>1. Repair first, then list</h3>
<p>Best when you have the cash or time, the engineer's scope is clear, and your house would otherwise appeal to financed buyers. You list with a clean story: problem found, engineered repair, permit, warranty.</p>
<h3>2. List as-is with a credit or price reduction</h3>
<p>You disclose the problem, include the engineer report and contractor bids, and price accordingly, or offer a credit. This saves you managing the project. Some lenders limit seller concessions or require repairs before closing, so confirm what your buyer's lender will allow.</p>
<h3>3. Sell to a cash buyer or investor</h3>
<p>Fastest and simplest, with no repairs, but typically the lowest price. Investors build in the repair cost, uncertainty, carrying costs and profit. Read our guide to <a href="/blog/selling-a-house-as-is-texas/">selling a house as-is in Texas</a> for how the option period and as-is clause work.</p>
<p>The table below uses hypothetical round numbers only to show how to compare net proceeds. Plug in your own engineer-based bids and local prices.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Line item (hypothetical)</th><th>Repair, then list</th><th>List as-is with credit</th><th>Cash buyer</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
